Id: lin024w
Severity: WARN
Category: kernel
Check: check_sysctl
Fix: Set kernel.sysrq to a mask without 8 and 64 (Ubuntu's 176, systemd's 16), or 0, in /etc/sysctl.d.
References: sysctl.d(5); Linux kernel documentation, admin-guide/sysrq (Linux Magic System Request Key Hacks)
Controls: CIS 4.1, 10.5; NIST CM-6, SI-16; ISO 8.9; CE Secure configuration

kernel.sysrq is 1, every SysRq function, or a bitmask that keeps the
dangerous ones: 8 lets anyone at the console dump the kernel's state
(tasks, memory, registers, blocked tasks) onto it, 64 lets them send
SIGTERM or SIGKILL to every process, or call the OOM killer, from the
keyboard or, as root, through /proc/sysrq-trigger. The functions are
bits: 2 console log level, 4 keyboard control (SAK, unraw), 8
debugging dumps, 16 sync, 32 remount read-only, 64 signalling
processes, 128 reboot and power off, 256 nicing real-time tasks.
Distributions ship a subset without 8 and 64: Ubuntu 176 (sync,
remount, reboot), Debian's kernel 438, systemd's 50-default.conf
(Fedora, Arch) 16; Alpine keeps the kernel's 1. 0 disables SysRq
altogether:
  # sysctl -w kernel.sysrq=176
